/* ================= t330 / t331 shops you can walk into. The owner: "These new restaurants and stores and workhands to be
   physical visual things you can see", "the staff you hire for stores should also be physical", "it shouldn't be stands, it
   should be stores; the stands are ugly and not well made", "when you upgrade stores you should see a visual difference
   exterior and interior".
   - MARKET ROW. The main road east of the Lamplighter is cleared into lots. Your own shop (a farm stand, a kitchen or a taproom)
     stands on the first one as a real building in the town's own style, with your shop's name over the door; in a versus
     world each player's shop has its own lot along the row.
   - INSIDE. Every shop has a room: a counter, shelves and displays for its trade, the people you hired at work (behind the
     counter, at the stove, pulling pints), and customers coming in, browsing, buying and going, while it's open.
   - UPGRADES SHOW. A painted sign: a proper signboard over the door (before it's a plank lettered by hand) and a price board
     and picture inside. Longer hours: a lantern by the door, the windows lit at night, an OPEN sign in the window and lamps
     inside. A second line: an awning, the second trade's goods out front and its counter or stove inside. The back room: the
     building widens with more windows and the room inside is half as big again.
   - THE TOWN'S SHOPS GROW TOO. A share's "building next door" is a second building in the shop's own materials and name on a
     free lot down its street, and a branch in Wickham is a building on Wickham's high street (no more market stalls); the
     hands you hire them work beside the keeper behind the counter.
   The buildings come from the same Aseprite building kit as the rest of the town (tools: t330/st30bld.lua), packed below. === */
